[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"297","stub":{"id":"297","section":"Patrick Creek Campground to Forks River Access","altname":"Multiple Runs","river":"Smith, Middle Fork","difficulty":"IItoV","states":["USA-CAL"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"767","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:45:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null},"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":"The river access point is at the confluence of Patrick Creek and the MF Smith River. To reach this spot, take the trail upriver from the upstream end of the Patrick Creek Campground. There are other access options in the campground area too. Located at USGS River Mile 16.5.","id":"101270","location":{"latitude":"41.874331","longitude":"-123.843611"},"name":"Patrick Creek Campground Put-in","distance":"0","photoID":"0","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"access","description":"The river access is signed along Highway 199. At USGS River Mile 15.2.","id":"104318","location":{"latitude":"41.86337","longitude":"-123.859206"},"name":"Sandy Beach River Access","distance":"1.4","photoID":"0","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"rapid","description":"This is the largest rapid between Patricks Creek and Gasquet. It is on a sharp left hand turn shortly after the river passes under the Highway 199 bridge. This rapid gets harder at higher flows. It can be scouted or portaged with some effort on river left.","id":"101269","location":{"latitude":"41.861279","longitude":"-123.874493"},"name":"Highway Rapid","distance":"2.37","difficulty":"IV-","approximate":true,"photoID":"0"},{"type":"access","description":"The river access is signed along Highway 199. At USGS River Mile 13.5.","id":"104317","location":{"latitude":"41.856134","longitude":"-123.886201"},"name":"Madrona River Access","distance":"3.2","photoID":"0","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"access","description":"Panther Flat Campground on river left can be a good early take-out for those boating down from Patricks Creek. At USGS River Mile 10.5. This US Forest Service campground is open all year, has a fee, and coin-operated hot showers.","id":"101271","location":{"latitude":"41.841742","longitude":"-123.932684"},"name":"Panther Flat Campground","distance":"6.19","photoID":"0","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"other","description":"The town of Gasquet lies between the highway on the south and the river on the north. The North Fork Smith joins the Middle Fork Smith near the upstream end of the town of Gasquet. A bridge crosses the Middle Fork just before the confluence and there are a few homes visible along the river banks here. Located at USGS River Mile 8.1.\n\nThe Gasquet Water Plant is on the river right side at the confluence, and a steep climb up the rocks leads to a trail then double-track road past the water plant to the paved Gasquet Flat Road. Kayakers and othe small craft paddlers often use this as a take-out. Rafters typically continue downriver to take out at the more accesible Margie's River Access.","id":"101273","location":{"latitude":"41.84782","longitude":"-123.969035"},"name":"NF Smith Confluence & Water Plant River Access","distance":"8.6","photoID":"0","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":true},{"type":"access","description":"The river access is signed along Highway 199 on the far downriver end of Gasquet. At USGS River Mile 6.8.","id":"101274","location":{"latitude":"41.842839","longitude":"-123.996205"},"name":"Margie's River Access","distance":"10.24","photoID":"889294","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":true},{"type":"access","description":"This river access is signed along Highway 199. At USGS River Mile 5.5.","id":"104314","location":{"latitude":"41.841992","longitude":"-124.013029"},"name":"Mary Adams Peacock Bridge Access","distance":"11.22","photoID":"884917","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"rapid","description":"Rapid located at USGS River Mile 3.9.","id":"104312","location":{"latitude":"41.835606","longitude":"-124.035797"},"name":"Cars","distance":"12.87","difficulty":"III","approximate":false,"photoID":"884919"},{"type":"rapid","description":"Rapid at USGS River Mile 3.3.","id":"104311","location":{"latitude":"41.830254","longitude":"-124.027519"},"name":"Brent's Hole","distance":"13.54","difficulty":"III","approximate":false,"photoID":"0"},{"type":"access","description":"Access located at USGS River Mile 2.5.","id":"104310","location":{"latitude":"41.821734","longitude":"-124.033971"},"name":"Middle Fork Gorge Access","distance":"14.34","photoID":"884911","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"rapid","description":"Rapid located at USGS River Mile 1.8.","id":"104309","location":{"latitude":"41.81529273","longitude":"-124.04090479"},"name":"Nozzle","distance":"14.94","difficulty":"IV+","approximate":false,"photoID":"884913"},{"type":"rapid","description":"Named for a hole in the rock on river right that is often underwater at typical boating flows. The entrance pushes boaters toward a center rock, forcing a quick decision and active maneuvering to either side—both of which are viable. Rapid located at USGS River Mile 1.7.","id":"104308","location":{"latitude":"41.81440276","longitude":"-124.04285166"},"name":"Hole in the Wall","distance":"15.06","difficulty":"IV-","approximate":false,"photoID":"884914"},{"type":"access","description":"On river right below the South Fork Road bridge. This is not often used by boaters because the Forks River Access on river left is much more easily accessed. Located at USGS River Mile 0.4.","id":"104307","location":{"latitude":"41.80120385","longitude":"-124.05373291"},"name":"Myrtle Beach Access","distance":"16.33","photoID":"884921","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"access","description":"This is the primary take-out access for both the Middle Fork Smith’s Oregon Hole Gorge Run and the South Fork Gorge Run. The facility consists of a single parking area with separate routes to each river—a trail to the South Fork and a road to the Middle Fork. Located between the two forks off South Fork Road, at USGS River Mile 0.3 on the Middle Fork.","id":"104306","location":{"latitude":"41.799362","longitude":"-124.056186"},"name":"Forks River Access","distance":"16.51","photoID":"884922","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[],"photos":[{"id":"889293","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"891933","description":"The trail down to the water at Margie's Access","caption":"Margie's Access","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe ©","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/889293","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/889293"}},{"id":"889294","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"891933","description":"Changing screen and sign","caption":"Margie's Access","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe ©","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/889294","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/889294"}},{"id":"884911","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"887600","description":"","caption":"Middle Fork Gorge Access Sign","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/884911","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/884911"}},{"id":"884917","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"887601","description":"","caption":"Mary Adams Bridge","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/884917","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/884917"}},{"id":"884921","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"887601","description":"","caption":"Myrtle Beach","poi_name":"","subject":"Eric Bessette","author":"Thomas O'Keefe","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/884921","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/884921"}},{"id":"884916","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"887601","description":"","caption":"Margie's Access","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/884916","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/884916"}},{"id":"884922","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"887601","description":"Boat ramp down to gravel bar at Smith River Forks Access.","caption":"Smith River Forks Access","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/884922","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/884922"}},{"id":"889295","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"891933","description":"","caption":"National Wild and Scenic River Sign","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe ©","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/889295","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/889295"}},{"id":"889296","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"891933","description":"","caption":"Middle Fork Smith rapid","poi_name":"Dave Steindorf","subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe ©","poi_id":"101273","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/889296","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/889296"}},{"id":"884912","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"887600","description":"","caption":"Nozzle","poi_name":"Nozzle","subject":"Eric Bessette","author":"Thomas O'Keefe","poi_id":"104309","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/884912","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/884912"}},{"id":"884913","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"887600","description":"","caption":"Nozzle","poi_name":"Nozzle","subject":"Grant Werschkull","author":"Thomas O'Keefe","poi_id":"104309","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/884913","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/884913"}},{"id":"884914","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"887600","description":"","caption":"Hole in the Wall","poi_name":"Hole in the Wall","subject":"Eric Bessette","author":"Thomas O'Keefe","poi_id":"104311","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/884914","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/884914"}},{"id":"884915","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"887600","description":"","caption":"Hole in the Wall","poi_name":"Hole in the Wall","subject":"Grant Werschkull","author":"Thomas O'Keefe","poi_id":"104311","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/884915","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/884915"}},{"id":"884919","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"887601","description":"","caption":"Cars","poi_name":"Cars","subject":"Thomas O'Keefe","author":"Eric Bessette","poi_id":"104313","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/884919","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/884919"}},{"id":"884918","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"887601","description":"","caption":"Crotch","poi_name":"Crotch","subject":"Eric Bessette","author":"Thomas O'Keefe","poi_id":"104307","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/884918","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/884918"}},{"id":"884920","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"887601","description":"Oregon Hole at lower flows.","caption":"Oregon Hole","poi_name":"Oregon Hole","subject":"Eric Bessette","author":"Thomas O'Keefe","poi_id":"101273","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/884920","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/884920"}}],"detail":{"id":"297","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"Depending on flows, you can find good whitewater within about 10 miles of the California-Oregon border and continuing all the way down to the confluence with the South Fork about 22 miles downstream near Hiouchi. The river is all roadside with multiple access points. The four runs below represent the standard options but multiple access points allow you to further customize your trip.\n\n*1) Patrick Creek to Gasquet, 9.7 miles, class III(IV) (Patrick Creek Run)*\n\nThe run from Patrick Creek to Panther Flat is a class III/III+ classic. You will encounter somewhere between 15 to 20 class rapids in the stretch from Patrick Creek down to Panther Flat with consistent action from start to finish.\n\nThis section also includes one class IV rapid known as Highway Rapid. Shortly after the river passes under the highway from south to north, you will come to Highway Rapid where the river makes a sharp left hand turn. The rapid gets harder as flows rise, but can be scouted or portaged with some effort on river left.\n\nPanther Flat Campground is the usual take out for this run but you can continue on downstream through Gasquet, past the confluence of the North Fork and take-out at Margie’s Access.\n\n*2) Gasquet to Middle Fork Gorge, 4.3 miles, class II/III (Margie's Run)*\n\nThis is a great run that starts out as class II from Gasquet. If you want to cut out this first section, you can put in at the Mary Adams bridge. Below this point the whitewater action builds with some fun class III rapids including the named drops of Crotch, Cars, and Brent's Hole.\n\nYou can use this run as a fun prelude and warm-up to the gorge downstream or take out at Middle Fork Gorge access site if you are an intermediate paddler.\n\n*3) Middle Fork Gorge, 2.2 miles, class IV/V (Oregon Hole Gorge Run)*\n\nThe short Middle Fork Gorge is often run by itself but you can start higher up for some warm-up. The run starts with some fun class III+ drops that build in intensity as you approach the Nozzle. This rapid is a long sequence that changes with flows and while you can get a good overview from the road it is wise to scout from river level. You can take out early on river left and scout the entrance and series of drops or boat scout through the entrance and grab an eddy on river right to hop out and pick the line through the main section.\n\nShortly downstream you will come to Hole in the Wall where the river flows along a dramatic cliff on river left. This rapid is visible from the road but as with the rapid above, you will want to hop out at river level on the right to scout your line and figure out how to best avoid the hole.\n\nJust around the corner and out of site from the road, you will come to the Oregon Hole. This rapid is of little consequence at lower flows but can build to epic proportions at higher flows.\n\nAfter Oregon Hole the run mellows out and you will soon pass under the bridge where you can take-out at Myrtle Beach on river right or Smith River Forks just downstream on river left.\n\nCheck out Oregon Kayaking River page for some [descriptions of this gorge](http://www.oregonkayaking.net/rivers/oregonhole/oregonhole.html).\n\n**Logistics:**\n\nThe Smith drainage is in the far northwest corner of California. From the coast, take highway 101 to Crescent City and then head up Highway 199 through Jedidiah Smith State Park and the community of Hiouchi which is near the confluence where the Middle Fork joins the South Fork. If you are coming in from Interstate 5, Highway 199 heads south from Grant’s Pass. Once you pass the California border, it’s only about 10 miles until you start driving along the river. Highway 199 parallels the river providing many options for access points.\n\nAccess Sites:\n\nPatrick Creek Picnic Area: Located at Highway 199 mile post 22.0 with a 350’ trail to the river, parking for 7 vehicles, and flush toilets. This is a take-out for the upper gorge or a put-in for the fun class III run downstream.\n\nSandy Beach: Located at Highway 199 mile post 20.9 with a 255’ trail to the river, parking for 10 vehicles, and vault toilet.\n\nMadrona: Located at Highway 199 mile post 19.2 with parking for 10 vehicles, and vault toilet. You can drive right to the river at this access point which also has 3 designated sites for camping. This access is just downstream of Bridge Rapid and can be used as a put-in for a short trip down to Panther Flat or a longer trip down through Gasquet.\n\nPanther Flat Picnic Area: Located at Highway 199 mile post 16.75 with a 90’ trail to the river, parking for 21 vehicles, and vault toilets. Camping is available at the Panther Flat Campground. This is a standard take-out for runs that begin upstream at Patrick Creek.\n\nGasquet Water Treatment Plant: This an informal access at the confluence of the North Fork with the Middle Fork. It is a standard take-out for kayakers running the North Fork. At Highway 199 mile post 14.3 in Gasquet, turn on to Middle Fork Gasquet Road. Follow this road 0.1 mile and turn right onto Gasquet Flat Road and follow it 0.1 mile to the bridge across the Middle Fork. If you park tight, you can get a few cars parked along the shoulder (please don't block driveways). To reach the confluence, you will need to carry your boat downstream river right of the bridge through the green gate, past the water treatment plant, and down to the confluence. It is a short walk for kayaks but not practical for rafts.\n\nMargie’s (Sims Camp, Shady Bend): Located at Highway 199 mile post 12.86 at the downstream end of Gasquet (just as you pass the highway sign marking the entrance to Gasquet where the river heads away from the road). The river is accessed by a 370’ trail and the site includes parking for 10 vehicles, vault toilet, and screened clothes-changing area. Gentle trail slope affords easy access for rafts and kayaks. The access point is a small mud/sand beach and the river is a deep pool at this point. Adjacent to and in view of highway.\n\nMary Adams Peacock Bridge: Located at Highway 199 mile post 11.9 on west side of bridge with a 280’ trail to the river, parking for 20 vehicles, and vault toilet. This is a good put-in if you are looking for a short warm-up prior to running the Middle Fork Gorge.\n\nMiddle Fork Gorge: Located at Highway 199 mile post 9.1 with a 55’ trail to the river and parking for 7 vehicles. This serves as a put-in for those who just want to run the Gorge or a take-out for those who are not up for it. If you want to take a look at the gorge, there is a small pull-out at Highway 199 mile 8.45. From this point, you can look upstream to see the Nozzle (although the view of the river right side of the drop is obstructed) and look down below you to see Hole in the Wall. Keep in mind that road scouts never give an accurate perspective on the gradient.\n\nMyrtle Beach and Smith River Forks Access: A mile and a half upstream of Hiouchi at Highway 199 mile post 7.2 you will see the turn for South Fork Smith Road and the bridge across the Middle Fork. Myrtle Beach Access, the take-out, is right along Highway 199 on the downstream river right side of this bridge and provides access on river right via a 55’ trail. The site has parking for 20 cars and a vault toilet. A second access where you can drive down to the gravel bar is available on river left. To reach it, turn onto South Fork Road and cross the bridge. Continue down the road 0.2 mile to the Smith River Access. A parking area and restroom is at the top but you can drive down to the gravel bar to load up (a good option for rafts).","averageGradient":"47","maxG